        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-34" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Steve McEwen, <a href="http://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/october_7_1977#comment-34" title="Comment permalink">May 21, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>Nice story.  My mom, who was a life-long Dodger fan and who passed away last year, let me stay home for the Dodgers' one-game playoff against the Astros in 1980 and for Game Five of the 1981 NLCS.  Two very different outcomes, but both games remain important and cherished memories for me because of the excitement that my mom and I shared for the Dodgers on those days.  Your account hits close to home for me.  Thanks.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-35" class="comment">
                <p><strong>v reskin, <a href="http://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/october_7_1977#comment-35" title="Comment permalink">May 23, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>Nice memory. It reminded me of something I hadn't thought about for a while, a similar experience, but in Temple (High Holidays) during the 1965 World Series vs Minn. Game was right when we were in Temple and I brought my pocket am radio with ear piece. When my Mom nudged my Dad that we were disturbing her and others I feared the confiscation of my link to the most important thing  in the world. He told me to turn it off and follow him out to the lobby.He then shocked me by telling  me to stay out in the lobby get scores and inning then come back in to update him. He started checking his watch and would give me a sign when I could go back out to get the score and inning again. Temple was never better.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    
        
        <div class="comments-content">
            <div id="comment-72" class="comment">
                <p><strong>Chuck Pizagno, <a href="http://thebaseballchronicle.com/personal_stories/october_7_1977#comment-72" title="Comment permalink">Dec 31, 2009</a></strong></p>
                <p>Oh does this story hurt. Remembering the pain of the day October 7, 1977. We in Philadelphia are baseball starved for a winner. But this 1977 Phils team looks good. It is a brisk clear sunny day and the Philadelphia Vet Stadium is loud and crazy. It starts well as Burt Hooten gets rattled by the crowd noise and with several walks helps get the Phils get a lead 4 to2 which we are holid as the top of the ninth starts. Our oustandign closer Gene Garber quicxkly gets 2 outs and 2 strikes on the next batter; the bases are empty and I am certian we are about to win the game so I reach under my seat to gte out the camera in 
my bag. I wanna a picture of the Phils celebrating win # 2 in game 3 of a 5 game playoff ! As I reach for the camera Vic D hist a fly ball to left field and the croud gasps as Luzinski touches and then drops it. The it happens in machine gun quickness. The Dodgers score 3 runs and the Phils go out meekly in the 9th. Along with 60,000 plus other fans my wife and I exit the stadium in silence. No one is speaking. We get on the subway and still silence. It is like we are ALL at the same funeral. Yes that's Black Friday in Phillies baseball history.</p>

